            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>General Theory of Relativity</strong><br>\nIn 1915, Einstein published the general theory of relativity, which extended the special theory to include gravity. This theory provided a new framework for understanding the cosmos and introduced concepts like the curvature of spacetime. It was confirmed by the 1919 solar eclipse, which showed light bending around the sun.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Physics</strong><br>\nEinstein's work on the photoelectric effect, which demonstrated that light is made of particles called photons, earned him the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1921. This work was fundamental to the development of quantum mechanics, a field that explains the behavior of matter and energy on an atomic level.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Escape from Nazi Germany</strong><br>\nAs Hitler rose to power in the 1930s, Einstein, who was Jewish, faced increasing anti-Semitism. He emigrated to the United States in 1933 and took a position at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey, where he would spend the rest of his career.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
