          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Einstein's Later Years</strong><br>\nEinstein's later years were spent working on a unified field theory, which aimed to connect all the forces of nature into a single framework. Although he did not succeed in this quest, his determination continued to inspire physicists around the world. He also became a vocal advocate for world peace and democracy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nEinstein was known for his humble nature and unique personality, as well as his distinct appearance, which featured wild hair and a love for casual dress. He was married twice and had three children. Einstein passed away on April 18, 1955, leaving behind a profound impact on science and culture.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Einstein's Impact on Modern Physics</strong><br>\nAlbert Einstein's contributions to science are still felt today. His theories laid the foundation for much of modern physics and technology, including GPS systems and aspects of space travel. His intellectual curiosity and problem-solving approach remain a gold standard for scientists worldwide.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
